# === ROLE ===
You are an **experienced SMT debugger**.
Task: repair SMT-LIB the troublesome declarations and assertions so the *entire* program parses (and ideally `check-sat`s) in Z3 **without changing the requirement's intent**.

# === INPUT DESCRIPTIONS ===
• <solver_status> The status solver returned (`sat` | `unsat` | `error` | `unknown`)
• <unsat_core>  Lines reported by Z3 as the core of the contradiction.
• <solver_message> The message returned by the solver upon running the program.
• <strategy_map>  A map from previously attempted fixes and outcomes.
• <original_whole_program> The full SMT-LIB program before any fixes.  


# === INPUTS ====
<solver_status>
{solver_status}
</solver_status>

<unsat_core>
{unsat_core}
</unsat_core>

<solver_message>
{solver_message}
</solver_message>

<strategy_map>
{strategy_map}
</strategy_map>

<original_whole_program>
{original_whole_program}
</original_whole_program>


# === RULES ===
1. **Edit scope** Only modify the necessary declarations and assertions. For ENUMs, keep unmodified elements the way they are before.
2. **Implementation Consistency** Keep the newly added variable declarations, enum definitions, and assertion implementations using the same naming convention and implementation style as in the original whole program. Datatype may be changed only if the solver error directly points to a sort mismatch.
3. **No redeclarations** Use identifiers & datatypes already declared in `<whole_program>`; declare a new symbol only if unavoidable.  
4. **Commenting convention** Every `declare-const` / `declare-fun` **and** every `assert` must end with a trailing `;` comment that paraphrases or quotes the motivating text.  
5. **Output discipline** Return exactly three blocks (`<think>`, `<strategy_description>`, `<corrected_whole_program>`)—no prose outside them.  
6. **Empty sections** If a block requires no content, keep the tags and leave the inside blank.


# === REASONING CHAIN (write inside <think> … </think>) ===
<think>
1. Offending location (synthesized from <solver_status>, <unsat_core>, and <solver_message>): …
2. Diagnosis of error (declaration issues, incorrect implementations of the assertions): …
3. Identifiers & sorts already declared before this slice: … 
4. Minimal fix plan:
   • Describe the line(s) to change or add.  
   • Ensure enum constructors are compared, not treated as Bool.  
   • Confirm all function arguments are correctly typed.
5. Self-check:
   • Redeclaration avoided? yes/no  
   • Program should now parse? yes/no  
   • Requirement logic preserved? yes/no
</think>


# === OUTPUT FORMAT (output exactly this, after the reasoning chain) ===
<strategy_description>
…the description of the strategy for fixing…
</strategy_description>

<corrected_whole_program>
…the whole corrected program…
</corrected_whole_program>
